  "etymologyNotes": [
    {
      "s": "makemeahanzi",
      "t": "A foot"
    },
    {
      "s": "dong_chinese",
      "t": "Pictograph of a foot. The meaning of this character has shifted over time and now means \"stop\". "
    },
    {
      "s": "shuowen_jiezi",
      "t": "下基也。象艸木出有址，故以止爲足。凡止之屬皆从止。",
      "w": 1
    },
    {
      "s": "wiktionary",
      "t": "Pictogram (象形) – a footprint pointing up, originally meaning \"foot\"; stylized as 龰. The derivative 趾 (OC *kjɯʔ) refers to the original meaning. Compare 手 (“hand”). Note that in oracle script, composed of 3 toes and a sole.\nCompare to 夊, 夂, which have similar shape in oracle script, but pointing down (toe pointing right), and 𡕒 (as in right side of 舛, roughly ヰ, toe pointing left), hence different evolution.\nCompare to 丑, which is the pictogram of an animal claw.\nEventually, note that the foot is represented with three toes, like the human hand in the characters 爪, 寸, 九, 又, and 彐."
    }
  ],
